## What is in here

| Path | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `src/__APP_CLASS__App.cpp` | UI, animation timer, LVGL buttons + slider, `REGISTER_APP_PL` |
| `src/__APP_CLASS__PadLogic.cpp` | Pad handler — records events for the GUI thread |
| `CMakeLists.txt` | ESP-IDF component; the PC simulator globs `src/` itself |
| `crosspad-app.json` | Registry metadata (name, id, platforms, requirements) |

## Install into a project

```bash
# ESP-IDF (board v2; a v1 board uses build_v1 / sdkconfig.v1)
idf.py app-install --app __APP_ID__ --origin <this repo URL>
idf.py -B build_v2 fullclean
idf.py -B build_v2 -DSDKCONFIG=sdkconfig.v2 build

# PC simulator
python3 scripts/app_manager.py install __APP_ID__ --origin <this repo URL>
```

New app directories are only discovered at configure time, so the clean build
is required once after installing.

## Making it yours

1. Rewrite `__APP_CLASS___create()` — it is a plain LVGL widget tree.
2. Rewrite `__APP_CLASS__PadLogic` — `onPadPress` is the pad callback.
3. Keep the split: pad callbacks run on the pad thread and must not touch LVGL.
   Record what happened, let the LVGL timer draw it.

## Publishing

To list it in the catalog: a repo in the CrossPad org gets the `crosspad-app`
topic; any other repo must be public and is added to `external-apps.json` in
[CrossPad/crosspad-apps](https://github.com/CrossPad/crosspad-apps) by pull
request (CP Tools: Developer tools → Submit an app to the catalog). Step by
step: [Publish an app](https://crosspad.app/docs/how-to/publish-app/). A
private repo works fine without that — it just stays yours.
